<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. Two string literals of one text are one object (P-171). So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are <code>true</code>. Two string literals joined by <code>||</code> fold into one (&ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;), so <code>("a" || "b") SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>true</code>. A float numeral converts where <code>RR64</code> is declared (&ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;), so <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code> is <code>SEQV</code> a computed <code>RR64</code> 0.5. The interpreter's library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and is <code>false</code>; row 923 holds the question of longer tuples. To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own <code>&#x2263;</code>, declared as <code>===</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
