<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as the curator&rsquo;s decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why, 8 November.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule's meaning: what <code>SEQV</code> answers stays as the batch made it. The text now spells the operators out, and it adds three cases where a worker would guess wrong. The curator&rsquo;s comments on this change raised most of it. At 00:35 UTC on 8 November the curator asked to check the Unicode spelling of <code>SEQV</code> and <code>=/=</code>, and at 00:58 the curator asked that the text give both spellings and tell &ldquo;strictly equivalent to&rdquo; from &ldquo;identical to&rdquo;. The batch's text called <code>===</code> &ldquo;the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>&rdquo;, but <code>SEQV</code> is an ASCII spelling too, and the text never showed the characters. It now gives all three spellings of strict equivalence, <code>&#x2263;</code>, <code>SEQV</code> and <code>===</code>; it says that <code>&#x2261;</code> is another operator; and it says that <code>=/=</code> is <code>&#x2260;</code>. At 00:44 the curator asked that the skill use &ldquo;reference object&rdquo;, the term of the specification and of peer languages such as Swift. The batch's cold read had put &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo; in its place, since the skill did not define the term. Change 49 now defines it, so the bullet on strings says &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;. A first wording of the fix called <code>&#x2261;</code> &ldquo;if and only if&rdquo; on booleans. The cold read ran it under walk, and walk stops on it, since the interpreter's library declares <code>&#x2261;</code> for no type. The text now calls it logical equivalence, the specification's words, and says to write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The cold read also found two cases that the text left to a guess. Under walk, two equal string literals are two strings, so <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>false</code>, where a worker who knows Java expects one shared string. And once change 49 took functions out of the reference objects, no bullet said what <code>SEQV</code> gives on two functions. A new bullet says it, from the specification.</p>
<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them. They wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language, and told workers to assert it as correct. Here that was walk&rsquo;s new string object for each evaluation of a literal. By the curator&rsquo;s rule, <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress, and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design. A behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row. While it lasts, that path&rsquo;s part, <code>interpreter.md</code> or <code>compiler.md</code>, notes it, never as a rule. The curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-171, gives the language&rsquo;s answer: two string literals of one text are one object, and a concatenation of two literals folds into one literal. So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are now <code>true</code>. The second decision, P-172, makes a float numeral convert where <code>RR64</code> is declared, so <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code> is now <code>SEQV</code> a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same value. The sentence &ldquo;Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule&rsquo;s: assert them as correct&rdquo; is gone. Ledger rows 943 and 945 now hold walk&rsquo;s departures from these answers, and row 945 the compiled path&rsquo;s, and the text points to the two parts that note them. The sentences on walk&rsquo;s <code>&#x2260;</code> and two-value <code>assert</code> left this rule: <code>tests-writing.md</code> keeps them (change 44). A cold read then fixed the wording. It names the fold&rsquo;s operator, <code>||</code>, with the example <code>("a" || "b") SEQV "ab"</code>, which is <code>true</code>. And it names the case of a tuple of four elements: the interpreter&rsquo;s library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so such a tuple&rsquo;s <code>