<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them, since they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language. This rule&rsquo;s example, &ldquo;<code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>false</code>&rdquo;, was walk&rsquo;s answer, not the language&rsquo;s. By the curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-172, <code>QQ</code> converts exactly from a numeral with a radix point, so <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>true</code>. Walk&rsquo;s <code>false</code> is now a defect, ledger row 944, noted in <code>interpreter.md</code> (change 42). The example is gone. The rule keeps its other case: a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code> answers <code>false</code>, since no coercion joins the two.</p>
<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29.</strong> Not a new rule: a name. With the batch, the object <code>IntLiteral</code> left the interpreter&rsquo;s library, and the two numeral types, <code>NaturalNumeral</code> and <code>RadixPointNumeral</code>, took its place (change 40). Each declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So the sentence says &ldquo;the two numeral types&rdquo; in place of <code>IntLiteral</code>. The orientation update made the change, and its judge said to install it.</p></div>
