223	<section id="l40" class="part item">
224	<h3><span class="num">Change 40 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>A numeral converts where it stands</h3>
225	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
226	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, lines 89 and 91, the paragraph that was &ldquo;Under walk a numeral has a run-time class&rdquo;, now two paragraphs</p>
227	<p><strong><span class="cut">Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</span><span class="add">A numeral converts where it stands (P-173).</span></strong> <span class="cut">The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code> (row 364). Walk answers <code>IntLiteral</code>'s arithmetic as a <code>ZZ32</code> (row 578).</span> <span class="add">A numeral's own type is <code>NaturalNumeral</code>, or <code>RadixPointNumeral</code> with a radix point (P-174). Both lie below <code>Numeral</code>, a sibling of the number types under <code>Number</code>, and hold the numeral's exact value. Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude names them <code>IntLiteral</code> and <code>FloatLiteral</code>. A place fixes a type when its declared type is a numeral type, or has a coercion from the numeral type, as each of the eight number types has. Such places are a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter and a declared return type. At a number type, the numeral converts from its exact value: exactly into the five integer types and <code>QQ</code>, and with one rounding into <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code> (P-76, P-172). So <code>u: NN32 = 5</code> binds, <code>q: QQ = 0.1</code> is <code>1/10</code>, and <code>(h = 0.5)</code> is <code>true</code> for <code>h: QQ = 1/2</code>. A numeral outside the type's range, or that rounds to an infinity there, is refused: by the checker statically, by walk when the binding runs, with 'Type mismatch binding' and the numeral. Walk gives <code>Infinity</code> for the second (row 967). Elsewhere the numeral takes its default: <code>ZZ32</code>, or <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit, and <code>RR64</code> with a radix point. Elsewhere includes an untyped binding, <code>Any</code>, <code>Object</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>Index</code> and an unbounded type parameter. So <code>n = 3000000000</code> is a <code>ZZ64</code>, and <code>Cell(0.5)</code> is a <code>Cell[\RR64\]</code>. The default stays the value's type: for <code>x = 0.1</code>, <code>q: QQ = x</code> is refused. A type parameter that numerals alone fix takes the narrowest default that holds them all. A <code>nat</code> or <code>int</code> parameter used as a value is a <code>ZZ32</code>, so <code>u: NN32 = n</code> is refused (P-173, P-40). Such a parameter of 2^31 or more is a <code>ZZ64</code> under walk, and raises an error on the compiled path (row 962).</span></p>
228	<p><span class="add">Write a numeral type only as the parameter type of a coercion or an operator. The checker refuses it as the type of a variable, a field or a return (P-174). A program's own number type receives a numeral through its own <code>coerce(x: NaturalNumeral)</code> or <code>coerce(x: RadixPointNumeral)</code>. Its body reads the numeral's getters <code>digits</code>, <code>fractionDigits</code>, <code>radix</code> and <code>digitValue</code>. A negative constant, as <code>-3</code>, reaches it as the type's prefix <code>-</code> on the coercion's result for <code>3</code>, so a type without <code>-</code> refuses it. Until the switch-over, a compiled program writes <code>coerce(x: IntLiteral)</code> or <code>coerce(x: FloatLiteral)</code>. The coercion gives one value for each place where the numeral is written, and each run of that place reuses it. Without such a coercion, the type refuses a numeral.</span> The library's factories <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> give a <code>Vector</code> or <span class="add">a </span><code>Matrix</code> only at the eight number types<span class="cut">, not at a user subtype of <code>Number</code>. Elsewhere they give a plain array, whose <code>+</code> and unary <code>-</code> fail dispatch under walk. Under walk, an inferred element type is a run-time class, so they give a plain array (row 721). Write the static argument.</span><span class="add">; at a program's own number type they give a plain array. <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> give the other departures from these rules.</span></p>
229	</div></div>
230	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule, beside a fact that changed. The batch changed how walk prints a type: it now prints its numeral classes <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> and <code>BigNum</code> as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside a static argument. The old example, &ldquo;<code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code>&rdquo;, named a type that walk no longer prints. A worker who saw <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> in walk's output would take the value for a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, and a dispatch on that type would then fail. The text now says that the printed name hides the instance. After the cold read, it says what to write where a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> is needed, the workaround that ledger row 364 records. It names the printed names outright, in place of &ldquo;the traits' names&rdquo;. And the sentence on walk's error messages lost a &ldquo;still&rdquo;, which told of a change rather than of what a worker sees.</p>
231	<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them, since they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language. This item did so with walk&rsquo;s class <code>Int</code> behind a printed <code>ZZ32</code>: its rule told workers what to write because of it. By the curator&rsquo;s rule, <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress, and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design. A behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row, noted in that path&rsquo;s part while it lasts. So the paragraph now says what a numeral is in the language, and its title says so too. The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>. Under walk, until walk runs the checker, the specification&rsquo;s revised text gives it <code>ZZ32</code>, or <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit. By the curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-172, a numeral with a radix point is a <code>FloatLiteral</code> that holds the exact value. <code>QQ</code> converts from it exactly, and <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code> where they are declared, so <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>true</code>. Walk&rsquo;s class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> (row 364), its plain arrays from <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> (row 721) and its <code>ZZ32</code> arithmetic on numerals (row 578) moved to <code>interpreter.md</code> (change 42). A cold read then fixed the wording. It states walk&rsquo;s integer numeral in order: first the specification&rsquo;s text, then what walk does today. It makes the pointer to <code>interpreter.md</code> a sentence of its own, with the rows. And it puts back the library&rsquo;s rule on the two factories at a program&rsquo;s own number type, which the first rewrite had moved out.</p>
232	<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29.</strong> A change of the rule, with new rules beside it. The batch built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on numerals, P-173 and P-174. The rule said that a numeral has a type of its own, <code>IntLiteral</code> or <code>FloatLiteral</code>, that under walk an integer numeral is a <code>ZZ32</code> until walk runs the checker, and it pointed to walk&rsquo;s departures (rows 364, 721, 943 and 944). Now a numeral converts where it stands. At a place that fixes a type, as a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter or a declared return type, it converts into that type from its exact value. Elsewhere it takes its default: a <code>ZZ32</code>, or a <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit, and an <code>RR64</code> with a radix point. A numeral&rsquo;s own types are now the Working Draft&rsquo;s <code>NaturalNumeral</code> and <code>RadixPointNumeral</code>, and the compiler&rsquo;s prelude keeps the names <code>IntLiteral</code> and <code>FloatLiteral</code> until the switch-over. The batch fixed the four departures, so the pointer to them went. A worker who followed the old rule would have written <code>IntLiteral</code> as a type, which the interpreter&rsquo;s library no longer has, or kept away from forms that now work, such as <code>u: NN32 = 5</code>. The second paragraph is new. It says that a numeral type is written only as the parameter type of a coercion or an operator, how a program&rsquo;s own number type receives a numeral, and that a type without such a coercion refuses one. The rule on <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> at a program&rsquo;s own number type stays. The orientation update&rsquo;s own checks added the place whose declared type is a numeral type, <code>Object</code> among the places that take the default, the refusal of a numeral that rounds to an infinity, with walk&rsquo;s <code>Infinity</code> in its place (row 967), and a size of 2^31 or more used as a value (row 962). Its judge said to install the section. After the skill&rsquo;s cold read, five more changes. Walk&rsquo;s refusal of a numeral out of range now names its message, &lsquo;Type mismatch binding&rsquo;, so that a worker can write the test&rsquo;s check. The sentence on a size of 2^31 or more names the parameter, where it said a clipped &ldquo;raises compiled&rdquo;. The text names the getters through which a coercion&rsquo;s body reads the numeral: <code>digits</code>, <code>fractionDigits</code>, <code>radix</code> and <code>digitValue</code>. A new sentence says, from the specification, that a negative constant such as <code>-3</code> reaches a program&rsquo;s own number type as that type&rsquo;s prefix <code>-</code> on the coercion&rsquo;s result for <code>3</code>, so a type without <code>-</code> refuses it: no sentence said what a negative numeral does there. And the list of open rows that ended the second paragraph is cut, since a worker could not act on row numbers alone. The pointer to <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> stays.</p></div>
233	</section>
