236	<h3><span class="num">Change 41 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Number declares nothing</h3>
237	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
238	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 99, a new rule after the rule on coercions, and now after the rule of change 51</p>
239	<p><span class="add"><strong><code>Number</code> declares nothing (P-165).</strong> Each of the eight number types, and the two numeral types, declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call on a <code>T</code> no method or operator that the number types declare, <code>asFloat</code> among them. Two numbers of different types compare by <code>=</code> only where a coercion carries one into the other: the library declares <code>=</code> for each such pair, so a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value are <code>=</code>. Otherwise <code>=</code> is <code>SEQV</code> (&ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;), and answers <code>false</code> without an error, as for a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code>. To compare values across types at known types, write <code>asFloat(x) = y</code>. In generic code over <code>T extends Number</code>, declare your function once for each number type instead. A program may also declare its own number type below <code>Number</code> (P-160).</span></p>
240	</div></div>
241	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. By the curator&rsquo;s decision, <code>asFloat</code>, and the <code>=</code> built on it, left the trait <code>Number</code>, and the batch made it so. Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, now declares its own <code>asFloat</code>, and <code>Number</code> declares nothing. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call nothing on a <code>T</code> that the number types declare. Two numbers of different types are <code>=</code> only where a coercion joins them. Otherwise <code>=</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and answers <code>false</code>, with no error. The old part said none of this: a worker would have called <code>asFloat</code> on a <code>T</code>, or expected <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> to be <code>true</code>. After the cold read, the text gives a pair that a coercion joins, a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value, which are <code>=</code>. It says &ldquo;declare your function once for each number type&rdquo;, since &ldquo;the function&rdquo; could be read as <code>asFloat</code>. And its last sentence no longer says why a program may declare its own number type: change 37 now gives that reason.</p>
242	<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them, since they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language. This rule&rsquo;s example, &ldquo;<code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>false</code>&rdquo;, was walk&rsquo;s answer, not the language&rsquo;s. By the curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-172, <code>QQ</code> converts exactly from a numeral with a radix point, so <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>true</code>. Walk&rsquo;s <code>false</code> is now a defect, ledger row 944, noted in <code>interpreter.md</code> (change 42). The example is gone. The rule keeps its other case: a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code> answers <code>false</code>, since no coercion joins the two.</p>
243	<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29.</strong> Not a new rule: a name. With the batch, the object <code>IntLiteral</code> left the interpreter&rsquo;s library, and the two numeral types, <code>NaturalNumeral</code> and <code>RadixPointNumeral</code>, took its place (change 40). Each declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So the sentence says &ldquo;the two numeral types&rdquo; in place of <code>IntLiteral</code>. The orientation update made the change, and its judge said to install it.</p></div>
244	</section>
